# Text files are LF in the working tree, on every machine.
#
# Without this, the bytes of a committed file depend on the `core.autocrlf` of
# whoever clones it. That is not theoretical: with autocrlf=true on Windows, a
# fresh checkout rewrote the display layer's manifest and themes with CRLF, and
# `display/test/generator-output.test.ts`, which compares the generator's
# output to those files byte for byte, failed on the clone while passing in the
# working copy it was written in.
#
# `text=auto` keeps git's binary detection, so a .wasm or an image is untouched;
# `eol=lf` fixes the one convention for everything it decides is text.
# Symlink blobs (mode 120000, the folder READMEs) are unaffected: git writes
# them as links and applies no conversion.
* text=auto eol=lf